# React Rules of Hooks (ReachDesk)

When editing React components, **all hooks must run on every render** in the same order.

## Required pattern

- Call `useState`, `useEffect`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`, `usePageHeader`, and custom hooks **at the top of the component**.
- **Never** place hooks after:
  - `if (...) return ...`
  - early loading / auth guards
  - plan-gating returns (`if (!allowed) return ...`) unless every hook is already above them

## Bad (causes "Rendered more hooks than during the previous render")

```jsx
export default function Page() {
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  if (loading) return <Spinner />; // early return

  usePageHeader({ title: 'Dashboard' }); // hook skipped on first render — CRASH
  return <Content />;
}
```

## Good

```jsx
export default function Page() {
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  usePageHeader({ title: 'Dashboard' }); // always runs

  if (loading) return <Spinner />;
  return <Content />;
}
```

## ReachDesk-specific

- `usePageHeader()` from `src/context/PageHeaderContext.jsx` is a hook — register it before any conditional return.
- Use optional chaining for title values when `currentUser` may be null during loading.
- After adding hooks to a page, verify: loading state → loaded state navigation without error boundary.
